## [ISSUE] Login-cache invalidation is skipped when the IDP's organization is disabled

**Severity:** HIGH — hidden, link-only or non-public organization brokers can keep appearing on the login page after the admin changes them, until the cache entry is evicted.

### The Problem

`update()` passes `idpById = getById(model.getInternalId())` as `original`:

```java
IdentityProviderModel idpById = getById(model.getInternalId());
registerIDPInvalidation(idpById);
registerIDPLoginInvalidationOnUpdate(idpById, model);
```

`getById()` always returns `createOrganizationAwareIdentityProviderModel(...)`. That anonymous subclass overrides `isEnabled()` to return `false` when the linked organization (or the Organizations feature for the realm) is disabled (lines 434-445). `getLoginPredicate()` includes `LoginFilter.ENABLED` (`IdentityProviderModel::isEnabled`), so for any IDP bound to a disabled org, `getLoginPredicate().test(original)` is `false`.

The cached ID sets are built from `idpDelegate.getForLogin(...)`, which is the default SPI implementation doing a JPA search on the persisted `ENABLED=true` column. It does not look at the organization's enabled state at all. So the cache and the invalidation check use different definitions of "available for login".

### Theory / Proof

The admin REST update (`IdentityProviderResource.updateIdpFromRep`, services/.../IdentityProviderResource.java:185-197) builds `updated` with `RepresentationToModel.toModel(...)`. That is a plain `IdentityProviderModel`, with `organizationId` copied from the representation and no wrapper. Scenario:

1. Org `acme` has public broker `idp-x` (enabled, not hidden). A login page render caches its ID under `<realm>.idp.login.ALL` / `ORG_ONLY`.
2. Admin disables org `acme`.
3. Admin sets `hideOnLogin=true` on `idp-x` (or `linkOnly=true`, or removes `kc.org.broker.public`).
4. `original` is the wrapper, so `isEnabled()` is false and the predicate is false. `updated` has `hideOnLogin=true`, so the predicate is false. The first early-return (`!test(original) && !test(updated)`) fires and nothing is invalidated.
5. Admin re-enables org `acme`. `getForLogin(ALL, ...)` still returns the cached ID for `idp-x`. `OrganizationAwareIdentityProviderBean.searchForIdentityProviders` re-checks only `idp.isEnabled()` (lines 75 and 80), not `isHideOnLogin()`, `isLinkOnly()` or `BROKER_PUBLIC`, so `idp-x` is rendered on the login page.

The per-IDP entry (`internalId`) is invalidated correctly. Only the list query is stale.

### What happens today vs. what will break

With the org enabled, the wrapper and raw `isEnabled()` agree, and the new test covers that case. The bug only shows up when an org IDP is edited while its organization is disabled. That is a normal admin workflow: disable an org, reconfigure its brokers, re-enable it.

### Research

The existing `getByOrganization` cache in this class avoids the problem by also invalidating on `isInvalid(orgId)`. Infinispan realm caches elsewhere in Keycloak (for example `RealmCacheSession` client/role queries) compute invalidation from persisted state, not from computed or decorated views.

### Recommendation

Evaluate the predicate against the persisted model, not the org-aware wrapper:

```java
@Override
public void update(IdentityProviderModel model) {
    IdentityProviderModel idpById = getById(model.getInternalId());
    registerIDPInvalidation(idpById);
    // use the raw stored state so the check matches what idpDelegate.getForLogin() caches
    registerIDPLoginInvalidationOnUpdate(idpDelegate.getById(model.getInternalId()), model);
    idpDelegate.update(model);
}
```

Also evaluate `updated` without the wrapper (for example `new IdentityProviderModel(model)` is not enough, because the copy constructor snapshots `isEnabled()`). Or, more simply, invalidate the login keys whenever any of `enabled`, `linkOnly`, `hideOnLogin`, `organizationId` or `BROKER_PUBLIC` differ between the raw stored model and `updated`.
